From: djsaunde <1245942+djsaunde@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 11:59:53 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 13/21] fast: optional screen-space linear shade and fog, as
 the RDP interpolates

The RDP steps shade colour (and the fog factor in shade alpha) linearly
in screen space; only texture coordinates are perspective-corrected.
GL interpolates every varying perspective-correctly, which on a large
ground triangle reaching toward the camera keeps the near vertex's colour
over far more of the screen. 1080's lit snow near the camera came out
15-45 levels darker than on the N64 (title demo 705-753, the main-menu
shot, Trick Attack).

GfxRenderingAPIOGL::SetAffineShading(true), before the first shader is
built, makes the shade inputs (vInput*) and vFog screen-linear on every
GL path (desktop, core profile, ES; no extension): the vertex shader
outputs each one times w, and w itself, all interpolated
perspective-correctly, and the fragment shader divides them, which is
the screen-linear value (the varyings are renamed ...Np by #define).

Not the `noperspective` qualifier: Mesa's clipper (llvmpipe, softpipe;
desktop GL and GL_NV_shader_noperspective_interpolation on ES) gives a
noperspective varying at a clipped vertex the screen-space ratio along x
whenever the clip-space x differ. For an edge lying on a side plane
(both ends made by clipping against x = +-w) the screen x are equal:
0 / 0, NaN shade, and the whole clipped triangle came out black (1080's
attract demo: black wedges where a background triangle covers a screen
corner). Perspective-correct varyings are clipped in clip space, which
every clipper does right.

Only correct with clipped geometry (the previous patch): a screen-linear
varying across a vertex behind the eye is meaningless. Off by default.
